/id #edb/ref 17592186520792} {:answer/id #uuid "25e92a54-1b79-51b0-9145-95bb94f1dc21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520845} {:answer/id #uuid "3c19642d-0d29-5db8-8698-690e13c0ca93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520897} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee1a542-97ea-57ea-832a-117266a654c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520977} {:answer/id #uuid "82dd3bce-a901-539c-9a60-4a9bc7a451d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520845} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea4243f-e8bc-5238-a430-24274b725da6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521362}] :question/id #uuid "7eff1b38-24fd-5e4c-adf3-ec6e1162e257" :question/math-academy-id "q-138484" :question/problem "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607fba4b-a3c5-53ce-9054-c4e5df896d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520982} {:answer/id #uuid "7644a604-cf73-5442-8725-be22ba80a563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521025} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca0e0bb-8a82-5517-b771-f93850f54c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521083} {:answer/id #uuid "a7695fdb-ee31-5096-a213-f3e9e5fbfec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521140} {:answer/id #uuid "bb902678-e8ab-5d83-8667-d11bab43ee55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521140} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b0c82a-9a7e-5567-921e-646b3756d6d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521432}] :question/id #uuid "92a0ecc9-adbd-5716-8a7f-f658ffcbaf41" :question/math-academy-id "q-138486" :question/problem "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Diagonal Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161904 :question/id #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" :question/math-academy-id "e-9830" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdc735895573e0d7c1eb7865233dd7b56a38aeeb9c76bba77fa669212262791d.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r ^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV_r \\, \\Sigma_r^+ \\, U_r^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma_r^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma_r.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/d7/d750c1c5030e0d1df7e8b14790e3f189e69346ca392d3d2885ad49db0e66b7e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e62f475-a411-5471-9109-4f0776a4b27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371757} {:answer/id #uuid "574d747d-2aa6-5905-8aa7-23b220e6387d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371758} {:answer/id #uuid "03f17a11-c9e6-5b87-859e-5ff94559f3f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371759} {:answer/id #uuid "8cffa160-d3a3-5a20-bf3c-7361ce0636c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371760} {:answer/id #uuid "41bc4d94-8584-56fc-a285-e1f7e9c02900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -13 \\\\ 6 & 6 \\\\ -13 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371760} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef734dd1-af99-5319-9529-058b09954f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371756}] :question/id #uuid "e9a9aba1-e21b-5ed5-95bf-a323e7389c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-136992" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{24}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 16 & -22 \\\\ -22 & 16 & 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91bac979-c3d6-587b-9e0b-37dae19db297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159357} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce847b-d79f-533b-b94a-3c520c287a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :a