r-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371808} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e124c6-5e8d-5d2a-98ef-899e8525e8f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371804}] :question/id #uuid "40e6735e-d054-576f-846f-84bde633ad42" :question/math-academy-id "q-138622" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 1