sition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae11b85-45e6-5dd7-8b03-2d6388de4723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510973} {:answer/id #uuid "673fceb3-6f45-5623-8b71-9c0dfa70f9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510999} {:answer/id #uuid "682473a8-ddf4-51d7-92e8-835c0c7d6bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511003} {:answer/id #uuid "97c1d397-ce2a-566d-87b1-9841a83b5ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511097} {:answer/id #uuid "e27eae27-fef7-5f3f-9694-1cc984a96420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510973} :answer-field/id #uuid "06233fbd-739a-5715-82d1-f750ac787a62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511311}] :question/id #uuid "e15227a9-4593-50e7-a6e9-0ebad0f88648" :question/math-academy-id "q-138617" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3134 :topic/title "Singular Value Decomposition and the Pseudoinverse Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125401} :activity/id #uuid "91d90bfa-8f19-5546-aba3-f391b0f30b02" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125783 :tutorial/content "The **image** (or **range**) of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is the set of all vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ that the transformation maps to. That is to say\n![](images/4c/4c89132f0fd9713f0e688f7cf00189bf4d3f27baddad032c74edf7b0a8c0d8ee.svg)\n\nThe diagram below gives a schematic representation of the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: V \\rightarrow V$ for a vector space $V.$\n\n![](images/b8/b859250756eb3870f9c1d4d7e6e78f044adf2b0c4cd94a87d01011879d668afc.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{0}$ is always in the image of a linear transformation since $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the linear transformation\n![](images/61/618376922e3c837dba3f742fd77340c2a3deee6cee099843232f85bcec5faf93.svg)\n\nNotice that, for example, the vector $\\mathbf b = [6,9]^T$ is in the image of the transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ since there is another vector $\\mathbf{u} = [1,1]^T$ such that $\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{b}{:}$\n\n![](images/51/51004801582ad1a6cddc389e07a4ac8d486c410ebacf264de14ac1a798a10c30.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "70bccafd-705b-5026-bdf8-ef7731dfcc77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3530 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f42af34b-8f66-5aee-9bd5-7a3de03f5cdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056"} :step/id #uuid "ddfd46cc-2853-59ef-ace0-92fabfa7af70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73"} :step/id #uuid "eb409d13-a534-5413-97fd-7e71ad7fbfad"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f"} :step/id #uuid "905da979-387e-5ded-9c78-3ede6dac59ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125819 :tutorial/content "In general, the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to the column space of its standard matrix $T.$ That is,\n![](images/6e/6e8b0992ade86b8e8a0c7d2227ff5473f5cae843f8e763e556c17654e7f1f143.svg)\n\nSo to find out if a vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is in the image of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we just need to check if the matrix equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent." :tutorial/id #uuid "f52415b1-727f-53a7-8502-857fdcb3b835" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4276 :tutorial/title "Determining If a Vector Lies in the Image"} :step/id #uuid "cb766bed-08ee-564c-a45c-c20f57e59be3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125821 :tutorial/content "In general, the rank of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equal to the dimension of its image:\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T}))\n$$\n\nIf the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ has the standard matrix $T,$ then the dimension of the image is equal to the rank of $T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T)\n$$\n\nSo, in general, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{rank}(T).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89b10851-3eed-5f36-a0ab-c9d0610e9b75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4382 :tutorial/title "The Rank of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "9f45ab6b-4ad9-5e42-8a3f-69ef72e1a03c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125403}}] :activity/title "The Image and Rank of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124748} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421459} :activity/id #uuid "fde39103-04be-5db5-af7e-1c744f6989af" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125116 :question/id #uuid "994a2790-60b0-54d4-bc90-ddef3472f156"} :step/id #uuid "395bdc16-fff7-5375-a101-0c055ace3fdd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421455 :question/id #uuid "59614e83-6cc0-5ed3-9c08-72ebbf1a6792"} :step/id #uuid "534ab39a-0dca-5848-9ca5-2f3c78f6a6dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421456 :question/id #uuid "a248fe65-f8da-5537-9754-7384902accff"} :step/id #uuid "81c74876-07ea-5bca-9d74-5b474bd5a78e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421458 :question/id #uuid "dca0d5db-2f8d-5d5d-b6ae-e11ba42097a0"} :step/id #uuid "ac55dbd9-0c6d-5e76-ad71-45c14185e732" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421457 :question/id #uuid "a744da17-c832-5e19-8de7-48116074536b"} :step/id #uuid "e917b105-2397-5433-ac40-7bf4300f0b36" :step/nex